---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const shaHref = toolPathBySlug('sha256-hash-generator');
const md5Href = toolPathBySlug('md5-hash-generator');
const idHref = toolPathBySlug('hash-identifier');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>How do you hash a file in the browser? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Choose the file, leave SHA-256 selected, and read the hex under the file name.
      The page hashes the bytes you picked. It does not read the file as text.
      A picture, a zip, and an ISO all stay binary. Runs entirely on your device.
      Nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
    <p>
      SHA-256 is the default because that is the checksum most download pages print.
      MD5, SHA-1, SHA-512, and CRC32 sit in the same menu for the other lines.
      Change the menu and the same file is hashed again. You do not pick it twice.
      Copy puts the hex on the clipboard.
    </p>
    <p>
      Paste the published line into Expected digest. A match means those bytes
      are the bytes they hashed. A length mismatch names the other algorithm.
      The file is not called corrupt just because you pasted SHA-1 into the
      SHA-256 check. The size in bytes sits next to the name so you can see
      what was actually hashed.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open the File Hash Verifier →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="sha256">
    <h2>What is a SHA256 file checksum? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A SHA256 file checksum is 64 hex characters computed from every byte in the file.
      Change one bit and the string changes all the way through. Two files with the
      same digest are the same bytes for any download you will meet in practice.
      The spelling sha256, without a hyphen, is the name people type into search.
    </p>
    <p>
      The empty file has a published digest. SHA-256 of zero bytes is
      e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855.
      The three letters abc hash to
      ba7816bf8f01cfea414140de5dae2223b00361a396177a9cb410ff61f20015ad.
      Those two strings are how you check that a tool is hashing bytes and not a story.
    </p>
    <p>
      sha256sum on Linux prints the same hex for the same file. So does shasum -a 256
      on a Mac. Windows certutil prints the same digits in uppercase. This page
      folds case, so a match does not depend on which machine published the line.
      The algorithm is the SHA-256 from FIPS 180-4, read in 1 MB slices.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Same bytes, same digest">
      <p>
        The checksum belongs to the bytes, not to the filename. Rename the ISO
        and the digest stays put. Edit one byte inside it and the digest moves.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="verify">
    <h2>How do you verify a download checksum? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Copy the line from the download page into Expected digest, then choose the file
      you saved. The page compares its digest with the first hex token of that line.
      When both strings are equal, the status says Matches. When they differ at the
      same length, it says the file or the expected line is not what you think.
    </p>
    <p>
      A sha256sum line looks like the digest, two spaces, then a filename.
      d7a8fbb307d7809469ca9abcb0082e4f8d5651e46d3cdb762d02d0bf37c9e592  archive.bin
      is one line, not a longer hash. The page keeps the hex and drops archive.bin.
      Gluing the filename onto the digest is a false mismatch. The strip is the fix.
    </p>
    <p>
      A Docker-style prefix works the same way. sha256: followed by 64 hex characters
      keeps the hex after the colon. Uppercase from certutil is lowercased before
      the compare. Whitespace around the line is ignored. What must stay is the hex
      itself. A sentence around the digest will not compare.
    </p>
    <p>
      Length is the other half of the check. MD5 is 32 hex characters. SHA-1 is 40.
      SHA-256 is 64. SHA-512 is 128. CRC32 is 8. Paste a 40-character line into a
      SHA-256 check and the page names SHA-1. Nothing is wrong with the file.
      You compared it with a different algorithm. Switch the menu and hash again.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Reading the filename as part of the digest">
      <p>
        The sidecar file.sha256 is a text line, not 64 extra characters of hash.
        Paste the line whole. The filename after the spaces is removed before compare.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="empty">
    <h2>Why does a 0 byte file still produce a digest? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Zero bytes is a legal file, and every hash of it is defined. The note appears
      because that digest is the hash of nothing. A partial download is the usual
      way a person ends up with 0 bytes and a confident checksum. The mirror is
      not corrupt. The transfer never wrote the ISO.
    </p>
    <p>
      Look at the size before you look at the hex. The page prints the byte count
      next to the name. 0 bytes, then a digest, means stop. Do not install that
      file. Do not write it to a USB stick. Download it again and wait until the
      browser says the save finished. Then hash the new file.
    </p>
    <p>
      CRC32 of the empty file is 00000000. That string is easy to mistake for
      "no result". It is a result. Eight zeros is the checksum of nothing, the
      same way the long SHA-256 string above is. The note stays up for both
      algorithms. A non-empty file hides the note, because the warning was about
      the empty download and not about hashing in general.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Trusting the digest of an empty download">
      <p>
        A match against the empty-file digest means you hashed nothing. It does
        not mean the publisher shipped an empty ISO. Check the byte count first.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="large">
    <h2>What happens when the file is larger than 32 MB? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      SHA-256 and CRC32 keep reading. Each slice is 1 MB. The running digest folds
      that slice and drops it. A 4 GB ISO never has to sit in one arrayBuffer.
      Progress text shows how many bytes are done, once the file is past a single
      slice. Small files finish before that line would help, so it stays hidden.
    </p>
    <p>
      MD5, SHA-1, and SHA-512 still hash in one shot. crypto.subtle.digest takes
      one buffer, and the MD5 code here does the same. Above 32 MB those three
      refuse the file. The note says so, and the button says Use SHA-256. The
      button switches the menu and hashes the file you already chose, in slices.
    </p>
    <p>
      The limit is a guard, not a claim that 32 MB is a large download. A phone
      tab dies when a script asks for a multi-gigabyte array, and the person
      sees a blank page instead of a digest. Refusing the one-shot algorithms
      is the honest path. Sliced SHA-256 is the checksum the download page
      wanted in almost every case.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="One-shot algorithms stop on purpose">
      <p>
        If the publisher printed MD5 for a huge file, hash it with a desktop
        md5sum. This page will not load that file whole just to print 32 hex
        characters. SHA-256 in slices is the check it will run.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="which">
    <h2>Should you use MD5 or SHA-256 for a download? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Use the algorithm printed next to the file. A 64-character line is SHA-256.
      A 32-character line is MD5. Guessing from habit is how a good download gets
      called corrupt. The menu on this page starts at SHA-256 because that is
      the line current release pages print. MD5 is one click away when the
      publisher still prints it.
    </p>
    <p>
      MD5 catches a short download and a bit flip. It does not hold up as a
      security check, because people have built two different files with one
      MD5. For an ISO you will boot, prefer the SHA-256 line when both exist.
      The <a href={md5Href}>MD5 hash generator</a> is the text version of that
      older checksum. It hashes what you paste, not a file.
    </p>
    <p>
      SHA-1 is 40 hex characters and is retired for signatures. Some old archives
      still publish it. Match the length, then decide whether you trust the
      publisher. SHA-512 is 128 hex characters. Use it when that is the printed
      line. CRC32 is the 8-character checksum inside zip tools. It finds accidents.
      It does not stand in for SHA-256.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unsure which line you copied? Open the <a href={idHref}>hash identifier</a>.
      It reads the length and names the algorithm. Then come back here, set that
      algorithm, and choose the file. The identifier does not hash the file.
      This page does. The two jobs stay separate so neither one pretends.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="upload">
    <h2>Is the file uploaded? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      No. The bytes stay in the tab that hashed them. There is no upload field
      that posts to a server, and there is no account. Closing the tab drops
      the file. The sentence under the file control is the whole privacy policy
      for this tool: runs entirely on your device, nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is why a chat window is the wrong place for this job. A model cannot
      see a file on your disk unless you send the file. Sending it is an upload.
      The digest is also not a secret about you, but the ISO might be a private
      build, a disk image, or a contract. Hash it here and paste only the hex
      if you need to show someone the checksum.
    </p>
    <p>
      Text is a different job. The <a href={shaHref}>SHA-256 hash generator</a>
      hashes the characters you type, including a trailing newline if you leave
      one. A file hash will not match that text hash unless the file is those
      exact bytes. Save the sentence, then hash the saved file, and you are
      comparing the file. Paste the sentence, and you are comparing the text.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="read-a-line">
    <h2>How do you read a published checksum line? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Start at the left. The first run of hex is the digest. Anything after a
      space is a filename, a star, or a label. BSD shasum lines put the algorithm
      in parentheses and the hex after the equals sign. This page takes the hex
      after the last colon, then the first token of what remains. That covers
      the shapes download pages actually ship.
    </p>
    <p>
      Count characters only when the compare says the length is wrong. 8 is
      CRC32. 32 is MD5. 40 is SHA-1. 64 is SHA-256. 128 is SHA-512. A count that
      matches none of those is a truncated paste or a line that still contains
      a word. Copy the line again from the publisher, not from a forum post
      that wrapped the hex in the middle of a sentence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then look at your own size. A 900 MB ISO that hashed as 200 MB was cut
      off. The digest of the short file will not match, and it should not.
      The mismatch is true. Download the rest, then hash once. The progress
      line on a large SHA-256 run is there so you can see the read finish
      before you read the hex.
    </p>
    <p>
      A sha256sum line is shaped as the digest, then spaces, then a filename. MD5 versus
      SHA-256 for a download starts as 32 hex characters versus 64. CRC32 versus a
      cryptographic hash is a bit-flip check beside a collision-resistant one. File checksums
      are those published lines. You hash a file online in this tab, and the bytes stay here.
      The job is to verify download checksum text against the saved bytes. An md5 file hash
      browser case is the 32-character line. A checksum uploaded file would leave the machine,
      and this page keeps the bytes local. Download verification is the Matches line. For
      example, hash an empty file. Input: 0 bytes. Output:
      e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855 for SHA-256.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Calling a short file a bad mirror">
      <p>
        Compare the byte count with the size the publisher printed. A mismatch
        on a short file means the download stopped. It does not mean the
        checksum on the website was wrong.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
